<p>Hail is the most common reason roofs in Central Texas are replaced before the end of their expected life. The tricky part is that a hit roof can look perfectly fine from the driveway. Ice striking an asphalt shingle can fracture the fiberglass mat, knock loose the protective granules and leave a soft spot that leaks months later. Effective <strong>hail damage roof repair in Temple</strong> starts with a careful, hands-on inspection that finds those impacts and tells you honestly what they mean for your roof.</p>
<p>Temple Roofers inspects hail-hit roofs across Temple and Bell County, documents what we find with photos, and provides written estimates for repair or replacement. We also help homeowners compare impact-resistant roofing options if they want a roof that stands up better to the next storm.</p>

<h2>Signs of Hail Damage on a Roof</h2>
<p>Hail damage shows up differently depending on the material and the size and density of the stones. Typical signs include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bruising:</strong> dark, soft spots on shingles where the mat beneath has been crushed, often without visible cracks</li>
  <li><strong>Granule loss:</strong> round or irregular bare spots exposing black asphalt, and granules piled at downspout outlets</li>
  <li><strong>Cracked or fractured shingles:</strong> splits radiating from an impact point, especially on older, sun-hardened shingles</li>
  <li><strong>Dented soft metals:</strong> dimples in roof vents, ridge vents, flashing, gutters and downspouts</li>
  <li><strong>Damaged accessories:</strong> cracked skylight domes, broken plastic vent caps, chipped paint on trim</li>
  <li><strong>Dented metal panels:</strong> on metal roofs, impacts may be cosmetic or may affect seams and coatings, depending on severity</li>
</ul>
<p>From the ground you can check the easy indicators: dents in gutters, AC unit fins, mailboxes and patio furniture, plus spatter marks on fences and siding. Our post on <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">how to spot hail damage on your roof</a> shows what to look for without getting on a ladder.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Don't inspect the roof yourself.</strong> Hail-hit shingles can shed granules that act like ball bearings underfoot. Look from the ground, take photos, and let a roofer handle the close-up inspection.</p></div>

