<h2>Hail Size and What It Typically Does to a Roof</h2>
<p>The National Weather Service treats hail one inch in diameter (about the size of a quarter) or larger as severe. Smaller hail can still damage older or brittle shingles, especially when driven by strong wind. As a general guide:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Hail size</th><th>Common comparison</th><th>Possible roof effects</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Under 1 inch</td><td>Pea to nickel</td><td>Granule scuffing; damage more likely on aged shingles and soft metals</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1 to 1.75 inches</td><td>Quarter to golf ball</td><td>Bruising, granule loss, dented vents and gutters</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Over 1.75 inches</td><td>Golf ball and larger</td><td>Cracked mats, punctures, broken skylights and vent caps</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Actual damage depends on wind, the angle of impact, roof age and material, so size alone doesn't settle the question.</p>

<h2>Impact-Resistant Shingles and Other Options</h2>
<p>If your roof needs replacement after a hailstorm, it's worth considering impact-resistant shingles. Products with a Class 4 rating under the UL 2218 test standard have withstood the impact of a two-inch steel ball in laboratory testing. They aren't hail-proof, but they generally resist cracking better than standard shingles, and some Texas insurers offer premium discounts for them. Ask your agent before you choose. Metal roofing is another durable option, though large hail can leave cosmetic dents. Compare the details on our <a href="/services/asphalt-shingle-roofing-temple-tx/">asphalt shingle roofing</a> and <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a> pages, and see <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a> for the full process.</p>

<h2>Hail Risk in Temple and Bell County</h2>
<p>Central Texas sits in one of the most active hail regions in the country. Most hail in the Temple area falls during spring storm season, roughly March through June, with occasional fall events. Storms often travel along narrow paths, so a neighborhood in south Temple may be hit hard while Belton or Salado sees only rain. That uneven pattern is why neighbors can have very different outcomes from the same storm.</p>
<p>Summer heat also plays a role. Shingles that have spent several summers in upper-90s temperatures and strong UV become more brittle, so they crack more easily when hail hits. A roof that shrugged off a hailstorm when it was new may not fare as well a decade later. Our <a href="/weather/">weather page</a> can help you check whether recent storms passed near your home.</p>

<h2>When to Schedule a Hail Inspection</h2>
<p>Get an inspection after any storm that produced hail around your home, especially if you notice dented gutters or vents. Don't wait for a leak; bruised shingles can take months to fail, and insurance policies typically have deadlines for reporting damage. If you decide to file a claim, our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claim-assistance-temple-tx/">roof insurance claim assistance</a> page explains how our photos and estimates fit into the process.</p>
